WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.451
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.453 --> 00:00:09.825
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.825 --> 00:00:12.326
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:14.758
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.321
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.323 --> 00:00:19.674
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.674 --> 00:00:21.428
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.811 --> 00:00:24.598
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.139
the start of another three-part

00:00:27.142 --> 00:00:29.724
mini-series, this time on three

00:00:29.726 --> 00:00:32.555
cities that shaped modern Britain.

00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:35.573
In today's episode, we are off to

00:00:35.575 --> 00:00:38.172
Edinburgh, to find out how a smoky,

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.796
overcrowded city that had just

00:00:40.798 --> 00:00:43.431
lost its own parliament became the

00:00:43.431 --> 00:00:46.092
intellectual capital of the world.

00:00:46.397 --> 00:00:48.607
In part two, we head south to

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:51.199
Manchester, the city that invented

00:00:51.211 --> 00:00:53.626
the modern factory, and then invented

00:00:53.628 --> 00:00:55.714
the ways of fighting against it.

00:00:56.091 --> 00:00:58.623
And in part three, we sail west

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:01.236
to Bristol, the city that opened

00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:04.232
England's door to the Atlantic, grew

00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:07.106
rich on the slave trade, and is still

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.326
arguing about that history today.

00:01:10.649 --> 00:01:12.749
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:12.751 --> 00:01:15.092
minute and get right into it.

00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:18.372
As I imagine you will know,

00:01:18.374 --> 00:01:20.901
Edinburgh is the capital of Scotland.

00:01:21.348 --> 00:01:24.485
It sits on the east coast, on the Firth

00:01:24.487 --> 00:01:27.702
of Forth, a wide inlet of the North Sea,

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.236
about 650 kilometres north of London.

00:01:31.936 --> 00:01:34.713
Today it is a city of around half

00:01:34.715 --> 00:01:37.405
a million people, famous for its

00:01:37.426 --> 00:01:40.436
castle, its festival, and for being,

00:01:40.438 --> 00:01:43.112
for many people at least, one of

00:01:43.112 --> 00:01:45.510
the most beautiful cities in Europe.

00:01:46.518 --> 00:01:48.673
It wasn't always like that.

00:01:49.502 --> 00:01:51.208
In the eighteenth century,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.307
travellers approaching Edinburgh

00:01:53.587 --> 00:01:55.708
would often notice the city

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.380
before they could properly see it.

00:01:59.148 --> 00:02:02.787
Hanging above it was a thick grey cloud of

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:06.253
smoke, rising from thousands of chimneys

00:02:06.571 --> 00:02:09.980
crammed onto a narrow ridge of rock.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.199
The Scots had a nickname

00:02:13.201 --> 00:02:15.581
for the city: Auld Reekie.

00:02:16.086 --> 00:02:18.092
In English, Old Smoky.

00:02:18.883 --> 00:02:21.332
And if the smoke didn't announce

00:02:21.334 --> 00:02:23.935
the city, the smell certainly did.

00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:27.918
Edinburgh in the eighteenth century was

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.967
one of the most crowded places in Europe.

00:02:31.628 --> 00:02:34.298
Around fifty thousand people lived

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.046
squeezed into roughly half a square

00:02:37.048 --> 00:02:39.660
mile, little more than a square

00:02:39.662 --> 00:02:42.357
kilometre, along one long street

00:02:42.571 --> 00:02:44.897
running downhill from the castle on

00:02:44.899 --> 00:02:47.356
its rock to the palace at the bottom.

00:02:48.197 --> 00:02:50.434
The city couldn't grow outwards.

00:02:50.663 --> 00:02:53.030
There was a defensive wall, a

00:02:53.032 --> 00:02:56.050
steep-sided rock, and a foul-smelling

00:02:56.051 --> 00:02:57.835
lake blocking the way north.

00:02:58.358 --> 00:03:01.593
So instead, it grew upwards.

00:03:02.658 --> 00:03:04.861
Edinburgh's tenement buildings,

00:03:04.863 --> 00:03:06.850
great stone towers of flats,

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:10.074
rose ten and eleven storeys high.

00:03:10.558 --> 00:03:13.651
Some reached up to fifteen storeys.

00:03:14.373 --> 00:03:16.301
For the time, these were some

00:03:16.303 --> 00:03:18.030
of the tallest residential

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.553
buildings in the world.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:23.586
And inside them, everyone lived

00:03:23.588 --> 00:03:26.495
stacked on top of everyone else.

00:03:27.042 --> 00:03:29.543
A judge might live on the fourth floor,

00:03:29.545 --> 00:03:31.851
a countess on the fifth, a shoemaker

00:03:32.085 --> 00:03:34.723
in the basement and a family of

00:03:34.725 --> 00:03:37.957
fishwives up in the attic, all sharing

00:03:37.957 --> 00:03:41.231
the same dark, winding staircase.

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:43.636
The most desirable flats

00:03:43.638 --> 00:03:44.876
were in the middle.

00:03:45.415 --> 00:03:47.843
High enough to escape the stench

00:03:47.845 --> 00:03:50.271
of the street, and low enough so

00:03:50.272 --> 00:03:52.131
that you didn't have to climb ten

00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:53.821
flights of stairs to get home.

00:03:54.822 --> 00:03:56.385
And about that stench,

00:03:56.387 --> 00:03:57.805
that horrible smell.

00:03:58.329 --> 00:04:01.701
The city had no sewers to speak of, so

00:04:01.703 --> 00:04:05.073
the custom was to empty your waste, of

00:04:05.073 --> 00:04:07.776
every kind, out of the window and into

00:04:07.778 --> 00:04:10.265
the street below, at ten o'clock at

00:04:10.266 --> 00:04:12.993
night, when the town drum had sounded.

00:04:14.309 --> 00:04:16.247
The traditional warning

00:04:16.249 --> 00:04:17.932
cry was "Gardyloo!",

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:19.993
which is thought to come from

00:04:19.995 --> 00:04:21.499
the French "garde à l'eau!",

00:04:21.887 --> 00:04:23.919
or "watch out for the water".

00:04:24.838 --> 00:04:26.712
If you were walking below, you

00:04:26.714 --> 00:04:28.524
had a second or two to react.

00:04:29.187 --> 00:04:30.714
And of course, "water"

00:04:30.716 --> 00:04:32.450
was a bit of a euphemism.

00:04:33.796 --> 00:04:37.806
This, then, was Edinburgh: dark, cramped,

00:04:37.808 --> 00:04:41.230
smoky and smelly, perched on a rock

00:04:41.506 --> 00:04:44.200
at the cold northern edge of Europe.

00:04:44.988 --> 00:04:48.225
It was also about to become the city where

00:04:48.227 --> 00:04:51.307
much of the modern world was worked out.

00:04:52.013 --> 00:04:53.686
The economics that still

00:04:53.688 --> 00:04:55.637
shape how governments think.

00:04:55.891 --> 00:04:58.074
The medicine that became the model

00:04:58.076 --> 00:05:00.065
for hospitals across the world.

00:05:00.572 --> 00:05:02.431
The discovery that the

00:05:02.433 --> 00:05:04.629
Earth is unimaginably old.

00:05:05.214 --> 00:05:06.888
Even Sherlock Holmes.

00:05:07.581 --> 00:05:10.637
All of it has roots in those crowded

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:13.523
staircases and closes, the narrow,

00:05:13.824 --> 00:05:16.340
smelly alleyways of the Old Town.

00:05:17.621 --> 00:05:20.039
To understand how that happened, we need

00:05:20.041 --> 00:05:22.275
to go back to a national catastrophe,

00:05:22.553 --> 00:05:25.509
and remind ourselves of some Scottish,

00:05:25.511 --> 00:05:28.699
English, and ultimately British, history.

00:05:29.935 --> 00:05:33.239
At the end of the seventeenth century,

00:05:33.241 --> 00:05:36.629
Scotland shared a king with England but

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.050
had its own parliament in Edinburgh.

00:05:39.985 --> 00:05:42.487
Failed harvests and famine had

00:05:42.489 --> 00:05:45.239
left it poor, and it was shut out

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.522
of England's profitable colonies.

00:05:48.106 --> 00:05:51.671
So it planned a colony of its own:

00:05:51.673 --> 00:05:54.816
Darien, in what is now Panama.

00:05:56.098 --> 00:05:58.597
From there, Scotland hoped

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:01.384
to grow rich on trade between

00:06:01.384 --> 00:06:03.198
the Atlantic and the Pacific.

00:06:03.968 --> 00:06:06.441
Nobles, merchants and ordinary

00:06:06.443 --> 00:06:09.327
townspeople invested their savings.

00:06:09.805 --> 00:06:12.411
Somewhere between a fifth and a quarter

00:06:12.413 --> 00:06:14.683
of Scotland's available investment

00:06:14.683 --> 00:06:16.631
capital went into the scheme.

00:06:17.935 --> 00:06:19.855
It was a disaster.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.339
Disease killed hundreds of

00:06:22.341 --> 00:06:24.627
colonists, the Spanish attacked,

00:06:24.885 --> 00:06:27.124
and England refused to help.

00:06:27.772 --> 00:06:29.503
Within a couple of years,

00:06:29.505 --> 00:06:31.234
the colony was abandoned.

00:06:31.974 --> 00:06:34.745
By the way, we did an entire episode

00:06:34.747 --> 00:06:37.362
on this story, episode number 564.

00:06:37.541 --> 00:06:39.350
So if you'd like to learn about

00:06:39.352 --> 00:06:41.275
this amazing story in more depth,

00:06:41.643 --> 00:06:43.485
that is the episode to listen to.

00:06:44.233 --> 00:06:47.785
But the thing to remember, as concerns

00:06:47.787 --> 00:06:50.963
Edinburgh, is that the losses from

00:06:50.964 --> 00:06:53.709
this failed endeavour helped push

00:06:53.711 --> 00:06:56.704
Scotland towards union with England,

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.108
which promised money to compensate

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.578
investors for their losses in the scheme.

00:07:02.463 --> 00:07:05.579
And sure enough, in 1707, the two

00:07:05.581 --> 00:07:08.507
countries became Great Britain.

00:07:09.903 --> 00:07:12.488
For Edinburgh, the deal meant

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:15.072
losing its role as Scotland's

00:07:15.092 --> 00:07:17.116
centre of political power.

00:07:18.142 --> 00:07:20.347
Under the Union, the Scottish

00:07:20.349 --> 00:07:22.399
Parliament ceased to exist.

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:25.093
There would be one parliament for

00:07:25.095 --> 00:07:27.363
the whole island, and it would sit

00:07:27.383 --> 00:07:29.583
four hundred miles away, in London.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.325
The parliament that met in

00:07:32.327 --> 00:07:33.810
Edinburgh in the spring of

00:07:33.822 --> 00:07:37.027
1707 simply closed its doors.

00:07:37.767 --> 00:07:39.619
A Scottish parliament would

00:07:39.621 --> 00:07:41.745
not meet again for almost three

00:07:41.745 --> 00:07:44.423
hundred years, until 1999.

00:07:45.532 --> 00:07:47.476
There were riots in the streets

00:07:47.478 --> 00:07:49.231
when the terms became known.

00:07:49.843 --> 00:07:51.747
Ordinary Scots felt their

00:07:51.749 --> 00:07:53.422
country had been sold.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.512
The poet Robert Burns put it in

00:07:56.514 --> 00:07:58.933
a line Scots still quote today.

00:07:59.508 --> 00:08:01.832
Scotland, he wrote, had been

00:08:01.834 --> 00:08:04.736
"bought and sold for English gold".

00:08:06.107 --> 00:08:07.808
Edinburgh was still a

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:09.591
capital city on paper.

00:08:09.829 --> 00:08:11.785
But it was a capital without

00:08:11.787 --> 00:08:13.880
its own parliament; the centre

00:08:13.896 --> 00:08:16.026
of power had moved south.

00:08:16.692 --> 00:08:19.778
So what do you do, if you are an

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:23.635
ambitious, educated, energetic city, and

00:08:23.837 --> 00:08:25.962
politics has been taken away from you?

00:08:27.398 --> 00:08:29.861
Edinburgh's answer, over the

00:08:29.863 --> 00:08:32.323
next fifty years, was ideas.

00:08:33.176 --> 00:08:36.089
The Union had taken Scotland's parliament,

00:08:36.091 --> 00:08:38.239
but it had left three things in

00:08:38.239 --> 00:08:41.213
Edinburgh: the Scottish legal system, the

00:08:41.215 --> 00:08:43.969
Scottish church, and the universities.

00:08:45.077 --> 00:08:47.308
That meant the city remained

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.459
full of lawyers, ministers,

00:08:49.719 --> 00:08:51.311
professors and doctors.

00:08:51.877 --> 00:08:54.862
Educated men with time on their hands,

00:08:54.864 --> 00:08:57.454
and, crucially, all living within

00:08:57.454 --> 00:08:59.388
a few minutes' walk of one another.

00:09:00.397 --> 00:09:02.847
Remember, this was a city where everyone

00:09:02.849 --> 00:09:04.929
was piled on top of everyone else.

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:07.846
There was no aristocratic quarter, no

00:09:07.848 --> 00:09:10.470
separate district for intellectuals.

00:09:10.759 --> 00:09:12.124
You could not avoid your

00:09:12.126 --> 00:09:14.000
neighbours even if you wanted to.

00:09:15.368 --> 00:09:18.459
And rather than staying at home in

00:09:18.461 --> 00:09:21.550
their cramped flats, these men met

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.813
in the city's taverns and clubs.

00:09:24.301 --> 00:09:26.953
They formed societies with names like

00:09:26.955 --> 00:09:29.676
the Select Society and the Poker Club,

00:09:29.966 --> 00:09:32.695
where judges argued with professors,

00:09:32.697 --> 00:09:35.728
and ministers of the church debated with

00:09:35.737 --> 00:09:38.195
men who doubted the existence of God.

00:09:39.652 --> 00:09:42.745
Out of this cramped, argumentative,

00:09:42.747 --> 00:09:45.484
hard-drinking little world came

00:09:45.485 --> 00:09:47.471
one of the greatest bursts of

00:09:47.473 --> 00:09:49.526
intellectual activity in human

00:09:49.526 --> 00:09:52.358
history: the Scottish Enlightenment.

00:09:53.564 --> 00:09:55.856
The novelist Tobias Smollett,

00:09:55.858 --> 00:09:58.464
writing in 1771, called Edinburgh

00:09:58.852 --> 00:10:01.805
"a hotbed of genius", a place where

00:10:01.807 --> 00:10:04.759
brilliance seemed to grow as easily

00:10:04.954 --> 00:10:07.459
as plants in a heated greenhouse.

00:10:08.347 --> 00:10:10.108
So who were the people

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.269
behind this burst of ideas?

00:10:13.048 --> 00:10:16.118
Perhaps the most famous was David Hume,

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:18.873
a philosopher who questioned almost

00:10:18.932 --> 00:10:21.911
everything his society took for granted:

00:10:21.913 --> 00:10:24.742
miracles, religion, even the idea that

00:10:24.743 --> 00:10:27.178
we can ever truly know anything at all.

00:10:28.274 --> 00:10:30.535
Today, philosophers regularly

00:10:30.537 --> 00:10:32.640
rank him among the greatest

00:10:32.653 --> 00:10:34.347
thinkers who ever lived.

00:10:34.699 --> 00:10:37.344
He also sounded like a lot of fun to

00:10:37.346 --> 00:10:39.989
be around; he was, by every account,

00:10:40.422 --> 00:10:43.027
cheerful, kind, enormously fat, and

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:45.929
one of the best-loved men in Edinburgh.

00:10:46.387 --> 00:10:48.313
He liked cooking, he liked card

00:10:48.315 --> 00:10:50.364
games, and he liked good company.

00:10:51.711 --> 00:10:54.840
One of his closest friends was a quiet,

00:10:54.842 --> 00:10:58.210
absent-minded professor called Adam Smith.

00:10:59.329 --> 00:11:02.587
In 1776, Smith published a book

00:11:02.589 --> 00:11:06.055
called An Inquiry into the Nature

00:11:06.264 --> 00:11:08.505
and Causes of the Wealth of Nations.

00:11:09.508 --> 00:11:12.889
It asked a simple question: what

00:11:12.891 --> 00:11:16.059
actually makes a country rich?

00:11:16.954 --> 00:11:19.420
His answers, about markets, trade,

00:11:19.422 --> 00:11:21.886
and the division of labour, became

00:11:21.886 --> 00:11:24.663
the foundation of modern economics.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:27.904
Almost every economic argument you hear

00:11:27.906 --> 00:11:30.698
today, from any side of politics, is in

00:11:30.698 --> 00:11:33.638
some way a conversation with Adam Smith.

00:11:34.328 --> 00:11:36.577
We also have an episode on him,

00:11:36.579 --> 00:11:38.680
by the way, it's episode 427.

00:11:39.766 --> 00:11:42.717
Then there was James Hutton, who was

00:11:42.719 --> 00:11:45.831
a farmer with an obsession with rocks.

00:11:46.941 --> 00:11:49.895
At the time, many educated Europeans

00:11:49.897 --> 00:11:52.603
believed the Earth was around six

00:11:52.703 --> 00:11:55.282
thousand years old, a figure calculated

00:11:55.284 --> 00:11:58.059
by adding up the generations in the Bible.

00:11:58.827 --> 00:12:00.705
Logical enough, I guess, if that

00:12:00.707 --> 00:12:02.583
is your main point of reference.

00:12:03.848 --> 00:12:06.326
Hutton looked at Scotland's rocks

00:12:06.328 --> 00:12:08.880
and saw something that didn't fit.

00:12:09.504 --> 00:12:12.181
Layers of stone that had been

00:12:12.183 --> 00:12:14.765
laid down, tilted, worn away

00:12:14.969 --> 00:12:17.257
and buried under new layers.

00:12:17.695 --> 00:12:20.295
Processes so slow that they must have

00:12:20.297 --> 00:12:22.966
taken millions upon millions of years.

00:12:24.631 --> 00:12:28.238
In June 1788, he took two friends by

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:32.146
boat along the coast east of Edinburgh,

00:12:32.524 --> 00:12:35.146
to a rocky point called Siccar Point.

00:12:36.145 --> 00:12:39.927
There, at the water's edge, two completely

00:12:39.929 --> 00:12:43.079
different sets of rock layers meet.

00:12:43.981 --> 00:12:46.644
One set stands almost vertical.

00:12:46.944 --> 00:12:50.164
The other lies nearly flat on top of it.

00:12:51.196 --> 00:12:54.463
For that to happen, the first rocks

00:12:54.465 --> 00:12:57.637
had to form, be pushed upright, be

00:12:57.638 --> 00:13:00.178
worn down by the sea, and then be

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.180
slowly buried under new layers of sand.

00:13:04.079 --> 00:13:05.613
You cannot do all of that

00:13:05.615 --> 00:13:06.962
in six thousand years.

00:13:07.907 --> 00:13:10.271
Hutton's companions understood,

00:13:10.273 --> 00:13:12.634
staring at that grey rock, that

00:13:12.635 --> 00:13:15.154
they were looking at evidence of

00:13:15.156 --> 00:13:17.830
a past almost too vast to imagine.

00:13:19.107 --> 00:13:21.810
One of them later wrote that "the

00:13:21.812 --> 00:13:24.758
mind seemed to grow giddy by looking

00:13:24.759 --> 00:13:27.386
so far into the abyss of time".

00:13:28.579 --> 00:13:31.023
In other words, staring at that

00:13:31.025 --> 00:13:33.310
much time made his head spin.

00:13:35.022 --> 00:13:37.347
Hutton's own conclusion about the

00:13:37.349 --> 00:13:39.461
age of the Earth became one of

00:13:39.462 --> 00:13:42.169
the most famous sentences in the

00:13:42.171 --> 00:13:45.045
history of science: "no vestige of

00:13:45.045 --> 00:13:47.748
a beginning, no prospect of an end".

00:13:48.736 --> 00:13:51.238
In plain English: no sign of a

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.657
start, and no sign of an end.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:58.220
He had discovered what we now call deep

00:13:58.222 --> 00:14:00.998
time, and it opened all sorts of new

00:14:01.002 --> 00:14:03.710
doors to explain the natural world.

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:06.428
Without it, Charles Darwin's

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:08.926
theory of evolution would have been

00:14:09.289 --> 00:14:12.020
unthinkable, because evolution needs

00:14:12.022 --> 00:14:14.372
vast stretches of time to work.

00:14:15.448 --> 00:14:17.917
And it wasn't just philosophy and

00:14:17.919 --> 00:14:20.761
science, ideas and theory; Edinburgh's

00:14:20.782 --> 00:14:23.242
medical school grew into the finest

00:14:23.244 --> 00:14:25.350
in the English-speaking world.

00:14:26.286 --> 00:14:28.875
Medicine at this time was still

00:14:28.877 --> 00:14:31.548
half science, half superstition:

00:14:32.167 --> 00:14:34.413
humours, blood-letting and so on.

00:14:35.591 --> 00:14:38.737
Edinburgh's innovation was to teach it

00:14:38.739 --> 00:14:41.884
systematically: lectures, dissections,

00:14:42.184 --> 00:14:44.816
and training at the patient's bedside

00:14:44.818 --> 00:14:46.950
in a proper teaching hospital.

00:14:48.092 --> 00:14:50.037
Students crossed the Atlantic from

00:14:50.039 --> 00:14:52.153
the American colonies to train there,

00:14:52.451 --> 00:14:54.443
then carried Edinburgh's methods

00:14:54.445 --> 00:14:56.559
home with them and built their own

00:14:56.568 --> 00:14:58.643
medical schools on the same model.

00:14:59.582 --> 00:15:01.935
The first edition of the Encyclopaedia

00:15:01.937 --> 00:15:04.041
Britannica was written and printed

00:15:04.042 --> 00:15:06.920
in Edinburgh, starting in 1768.

00:15:07.536 --> 00:15:09.610
It was nothing less than an

00:15:09.612 --> 00:15:11.683
attempt to gather all human

00:15:11.683 --> 00:15:13.701
knowledge into a single work.

00:15:15.504 --> 00:15:17.454
Word of all this spread

00:15:17.456 --> 00:15:19.149
far beyond Scotland.

00:15:19.951 --> 00:15:22.126
Benjamin Franklin, one of the

00:15:22.128 --> 00:15:24.525
founding fathers, visited twice,

00:15:24.701 --> 00:15:27.101
and became friends with Hume, Smith

00:15:27.103 --> 00:15:29.569
and half the professors in the city.

00:15:30.699 --> 00:15:33.239
He later wrote that his six weeks

00:15:33.241 --> 00:15:35.626
in Scotland had been "six weeks

00:15:35.636 --> 00:15:37.350
of the densest happiness I have

00:15:37.352 --> 00:15:39.175
met with in any part of my life".

00:15:40.328 --> 00:15:42.715
Even Voltaire, the great French

00:15:42.717 --> 00:15:45.255
writer, admitted, in his slightly

00:15:45.269 --> 00:15:47.404
mocking way, that Europe now

00:15:47.406 --> 00:15:49.768
looked to Scotland for ideas on

00:15:49.824 --> 00:15:52.319
everything from poetry to gardening.

00:15:53.183 --> 00:15:55.779
Fifty years earlier, Edinburgh had been

00:15:55.781 --> 00:15:58.108
the humiliated capital of a country

00:15:58.345 --> 00:16:00.805
badly damaged by a failed colony.

00:16:01.547 --> 00:16:03.595
Now it was being compared

00:16:03.597 --> 00:16:05.070
to ancient Athens.

00:16:06.528 --> 00:16:08.240
There was just one problem.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:13.097
The city itself was still a medieval slum.

00:16:14.098 --> 00:16:16.090
The philosophers might have been

00:16:16.092 --> 00:16:18.020
discussing how human beings and

00:16:18.022 --> 00:16:20.250
society could be improved without

00:16:20.252 --> 00:16:22.411
limit, but they were doing it in

00:16:22.485 --> 00:16:25.292
taverns down muddy alleyways, dodging

00:16:25.294 --> 00:16:27.644
falling sewage on the way home.

00:16:29.135 --> 00:16:31.695
While these ideas were developing,

00:16:31.697 --> 00:16:33.878
the city itself was changing.

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:39.039
By the 1750s, its leaders had had enough.

00:16:39.696 --> 00:16:42.644
Edinburgh, they decided, needed a

00:16:42.646 --> 00:16:45.682
home worthy of its new reputation.

00:16:46.778 --> 00:16:49.905
In 1766, the town council announced

00:16:49.907 --> 00:16:52.228
a competition to design an

00:16:52.228 --> 00:16:55.192
entirely new town, on open farmland

00:16:55.194 --> 00:16:57.817
across the valley to the north.

00:16:58.826 --> 00:17:00.814
Several designs were submitted.

00:17:01.392 --> 00:17:03.703
The winner was an unknown local

00:17:03.705 --> 00:17:05.864
architect called James Craig.

00:17:06.431 --> 00:17:08.040
He was twenty-six years old.

00:17:09.509 --> 00:17:11.496
His plan was everything

00:17:11.498 --> 00:17:13.311
the Old Town was not.

00:17:14.302 --> 00:17:16.962
Instead of a tangle of dark closes,

00:17:16.964 --> 00:17:19.927
there would be a simple, rational grid:

00:17:20.568 --> 00:17:23.145
three grand parallel streets, with

00:17:23.147 --> 00:17:25.798
an elegant square at each end, wide

00:17:25.799 --> 00:17:29.229
enough for light and air and carriages.

00:17:30.294 --> 00:17:32.910
Even the street names were a statement

00:17:32.912 --> 00:17:34.975
of loyalty to the new Britain.

00:17:35.629 --> 00:17:37.364
George Street, for the king.

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.083
Hanover Street, for his royal family.

00:17:40.491 --> 00:17:42.424
Princes Street, for his sons.

00:17:43.613 --> 00:17:46.042
The stinking lake below the castle

00:17:46.044 --> 00:17:48.256
was drained, and a great bridge

00:17:48.364 --> 00:17:50.532
was thrown across the valley to

00:17:50.534 --> 00:17:52.909
connect the old city with the new.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.766
And across that bridge, over the

00:17:56.768 --> 00:17:59.110
following decades, walked the

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:01.369
entire Edinburgh establishment.

00:18:02.059 --> 00:18:04.393
Lawyers, professors, merchants

00:18:04.395 --> 00:18:06.416
and aristocrats moved into

00:18:06.417 --> 00:18:07.892
the new terraced houses.

00:18:08.537 --> 00:18:11.104
David Hume was among the early

00:18:11.106 --> 00:18:13.843
residents, moving there in 1771.

00:18:15.059 --> 00:18:17.288
The New Town was, and still is,

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:19.588
one of the most beautiful pieces

00:18:19.722 --> 00:18:21.155
of city planning in Europe.

00:18:21.956 --> 00:18:24.262
Together with the Old Town, it is

00:18:24.264 --> 00:18:26.567
now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

00:18:27.496 --> 00:18:30.450
But the move came with a cost.

00:18:31.183 --> 00:18:33.399
When the wealthy crossed the bridge,

00:18:33.401 --> 00:18:35.369
they took their money with them.

00:18:36.148 --> 00:18:38.803
The Old Town, now abandoned by

00:18:38.805 --> 00:18:41.634
the rich, slowly rotted into one

00:18:41.646 --> 00:18:43.346
of the worst slums in Britain.

00:18:44.336 --> 00:18:47.140
Those same tall tenements that had

00:18:47.142 --> 00:18:49.861
once housed countesses and judges

00:18:50.322 --> 00:18:52.753
filled up with the desperately poor.

00:18:53.923 --> 00:18:56.681
The old city had forced every class

00:18:56.683 --> 00:18:59.360
of society up the same staircases,

00:18:59.496 --> 00:19:01.058
into the same conversations.

00:19:01.885 --> 00:19:04.179
In the New Town, a gentleman could

00:19:04.181 --> 00:19:06.405
pass his entire week without ever

00:19:06.406 --> 00:19:08.845
meeting anyone below his own rank.

00:19:10.073 --> 00:19:12.910
And this golden age wouldn't last

00:19:12.912 --> 00:19:15.918
forever; it started to fade towards

00:19:15.919 --> 00:19:17.742
the end of the eighteenth century.

00:19:18.463 --> 00:19:23.074
Hume died in 1776, Smith in 1790.

00:19:24.109 --> 00:19:26.182
By the middle of the nineteenth

00:19:26.184 --> 00:19:28.188
century, the centre of British

00:19:28.346 --> 00:19:30.834
intellectual life had moved elsewhere.

00:19:31.744 --> 00:19:34.084
But Edinburgh's influence had already

00:19:34.086 --> 00:19:36.551
spread far beyond Scotland, through its

00:19:36.794 --> 00:19:39.657
books, its students and their ideas.

00:19:40.819 --> 00:19:43.413
And the city kept producing surprises

00:19:43.415 --> 00:19:46.147
long after its Enlightenment had ended.

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:48.993
A century later, a young medical

00:19:48.995 --> 00:19:51.746
student at Edinburgh sat in the lectures

00:19:51.769 --> 00:19:54.467
of a surgeon called Dr Joseph Bell.

00:19:55.216 --> 00:19:58.632
Bell liked to astonish his students

00:19:58.634 --> 00:20:01.950
by diagnosing patients before they

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:04.001
had said a word, reading their

00:20:04.003 --> 00:20:06.598
profession and their habits from their

00:20:06.609 --> 00:20:09.430
hands, their walk and their clothes.

00:20:10.513 --> 00:20:12.173
That student's name

00:20:12.175 --> 00:20:14.183
was Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:20:14.877 --> 00:20:17.243
When he later invented a detective

00:20:17.245 --> 00:20:19.400
called Sherlock Holmes, he knew

00:20:19.401 --> 00:20:21.045
exactly who he was describing.

00:20:22.012 --> 00:20:24.421
"It is most certainly to you that I owe

00:20:24.423 --> 00:20:26.953
Sherlock Holmes", he later wrote to Bell.

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:30.762
Edinburgh has kept its reputation

00:20:30.764 --> 00:20:32.607
as a city of culture, too.

00:20:33.473 --> 00:20:36.357
Since 1947, it has hosted the Edinburgh

00:20:36.359 --> 00:20:38.872
Festival and its Fringe, which has

00:20:38.872 --> 00:20:41.721
become the world's largest arts festival.

00:20:42.807 --> 00:20:44.897
Today, more than three hundred

00:20:44.899 --> 00:20:47.126
years after the Union, Edinburgh

00:20:47.473 --> 00:20:48.855
has its parliament back.

00:20:49.925 --> 00:20:52.965
Scotland's politicians once again argue

00:20:52.967 --> 00:20:55.927
on the Royal Mile, a few minutes' walk

00:20:56.167 --> 00:20:58.209
from where Hume and Smith are buried.

00:20:59.110 --> 00:21:01.199
If you have been to Edinburgh, and

00:21:01.201 --> 00:21:03.165
you've been lucky enough to have

00:21:03.166 --> 00:21:05.859
had a dry, sunny day, you will know

00:21:05.861 --> 00:21:08.476
that it is one of the great cities

00:21:08.477 --> 00:21:11.050
of Britain, perhaps even the world.

00:21:11.723 --> 00:21:14.688
It is no longer smoky, no longer smelly,

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:17.727
and fortunately, you need not worry about

00:21:17.738 --> 00:21:20.050
listening out for shouts of "Gardyloo!"

00:21:21.649 --> 00:21:23.806
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:21:23.808 --> 00:21:25.828
episode, the first part of our

00:21:25.838 --> 00:21:27.759
three-part mini-series on three

00:21:27.761 --> 00:21:29.866
cities that shaped modern Britain.

00:21:30.391 --> 00:21:33.001
Next up we'll be moving to Manchester,

00:21:33.003 --> 00:21:35.198
the shock city of the Industrial

00:21:35.198 --> 00:21:37.690
Revolution, where the modern factory, the

00:21:37.692 --> 00:21:40.122
modern city and modern protests were all

00:21:40.122 --> 00:21:42.474
invented at more or less the same time.

00:21:42.747 --> 00:21:45.115
And in part three, we'll head south

00:21:45.117 --> 00:21:47.415
to Bristol, from the first English

00:21:47.422 --> 00:21:49.715
voyage to North America to a trade

00:21:49.717 --> 00:21:51.874
in human beings whose legacy the

00:21:51.874 --> 00:21:53.801
city is still wrestling with today.

00:21:54.555 --> 00:21:57.122
We also have episodes on the Darién

00:21:57.124 --> 00:21:59.762
Scheme, which is episode number 564,

00:22:00.019 --> 00:22:03.084
on Adam Smith, which is episode number

00:22:03.086 --> 00:22:05.745
427, so you can really do a binge

00:22:05.764 --> 00:22:07.963
listen on the story of Scotland.

00:22:08.732 --> 00:22:11.228
You've been listening to English Learning

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:13.541
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:16.529
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:22:16.531 --> 00:22:18.969
I'll catch you in the next episode.
